# Go statement-coverage floors, enforced by scripts/coverage.sh in CI.
#
#   package <import path>  <pct>   floor for a whole package
#   file    <repo path>    <pct>   floor for one file
#
# Package floors are a ratchet, set a few points below current coverage so an
# ordinary change does not trip them: raise the floor when you raise the
# coverage. Every package that reports coverage needs an entry — a package with
# no floor fails the gate, so new code cannot land ungated.
#
# File floors are not a ratchet. They are pinned at 100 on the security-critical
# path (auth, the audit ledger, the approval gate, secret handling, signature
# verification) because a package floor averages those files with everything
# around them: internal/auth can sit above its package floor while an uncovered
# branch in the RBAC check ships. A branch there is an unproven security control,
# so the file is read on its own.

# ── security-critical: per-file 100%, no ratchet ──────────────────────────────
file    internal/auth/jwt.go             100   # JWT mint + verify
file    internal/auth/middleware.go      100   # request auth, bearer + WS subprotocol
file    internal/auth/rbac.go            100   # org role → permitted action
file    internal/auth/workspace_role.go  100   # per-workspace role resolution
file    internal/secrets/encrypt.go      100   # variable secret envelope
file    internal/service/audit.go        100   # the audit ledger

# approval.go is the one file on this list not yet at 100, and the gap is not
# untested logic — every decision path, status guard and transition is covered.
# What is left is the `if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(...) }` wrap around each
# pgx call *inside* the approval transaction. Reaching those means making one
# statement fail while the others succeed on a connection already holding row
# locks, which the service cannot express: it takes a concrete *repository.Queries
# and *pgxpool.Pool, so there is nothing to inject a failure through.
#
# Closing it is a seam change to the approval write path — the narrow-interface
# treatment internal/auth already uses for WorkspaceRoleResolver — and that is a
# design change to the most safety-critical transaction in the product, not a
# side effect of installing this gate. Floored at the measured value so it
# ratchets, and raised to 100 when the seam lands.
file    internal/service/approval.go     82
file    internal/vcs/github.go           100   # webhook signature verification

